const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What are the bowers in Euchre?',
    a: 'The bowers are the two most powerful trump cards. The right bower is the Jack of the trump suit — the highest card in the game. The left bower is the Jack of the same colour as trump (for example, the Jack of diamonds when hearts are trump); it counts as a trump and ranks just below the right bower.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does "ordering up" mean in Euchre?',
    a: 'After the deal, one card is turned face up. In the first round of bidding, a player can "order it up", making that card’s suit trump and forcing the dealer to pick the card up and discard one. If everyone passes, a second round lets a player name a different suit as trump.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does going alone work?',
    a: 'The player who calls trump may choose to go alone. Their partner sits out the hand, so it’s one player against two. Sweeping all five tricks alone scores 4 points — the biggest single hand in Euchre. Taking only 3 or 4 tricks alone still scores just 1, the same as a normal make, so go alone only when you can realistically win them all.',
  },
];

