>
  <p>
    Euchre is a fast, punchy partnership game played with just 24 cards. Its signature quirks — a
    trump suit chosen each hand and two Jacks (the "bowers") that outrank everything — make it feel
    different from any other trick-taking game. The team that names trump must take at least three of
    five tricks, and the first side to <strong>10</strong> wins. This page is a complete reference. To
    learn by doing, you can <a href="/euchre/">play Euchre</a> now with a computer partner.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Euchre?</h2>
  <p>
    Euchre is a four-player game in two <strong>partnerships</strong> — you and the player across the
    table against the other two. It uses a stripped-down deck and a trump suit that changes every
    hand. Each hand is only five tricks long, so games move quickly and the lead can swing fast.
  </p>

  <h2>The deck</h2>
  <p>
    Euchre uses a <strong>24-card deck</strong>: only the <strong>9, 10, Jack, Queen, King and
    Ace</strong> of each suit. Everything from the 2 through the 8 is removed. Aces are high (except
    where the bowers take over in the trump suit, below).
  </p>

  <h2>The deal</h2>
  <p>
    Each player is dealt <strong>five cards</strong>. The next card is turned <strong>face up</strong>
    on the table — this "up-card" drives the first round of bidding — and the remaining three cards (the
    "kitty") are set aside face down.
  </p>

  <h2>The bowers: trump's special ranks</h2>
  <p>
    In the trump suit, two Jacks outrank everything else:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Right bower:</strong> the <strong>Jack of the trump suit</strong> — the single highest
      card in the game.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Left bower:</strong> the <strong>Jack of the other suit of the same colour</strong>
      (for example, the Jack of diamonds when hearts are trump). It counts as a trump card — not as
      its printed suit — and ranks just below the right bower.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Below the two bowers, the trump suit runs <strong>Ace, King, Queen, 10, 9</strong>. Non-trump
    suits rank normally: Ace high down to the 9.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing trump: ordering up and naming</h2>
  <p>Bidding happens in up to two rounds, starting to the dealer's left:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Round one — order it up.</strong> Each player may accept the up-card's suit as trump by
      "ordering it up", or pass. If someone orders up, that suit is trump and the
      <strong>dealer picks up the up-card</strong> and discards one card.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Round two — name a suit.</strong> If all four pass, the up-card is turned down and each
      player may now <strong>name a different suit</strong> as trump, or pass. (On CardHearth the
      dealer must name a suit rather than pass it out, so trump is always decided.)
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>The team that selects trump are the <strong>makers</strong>; they must take at least three tricks.</p>

  <h2>Going alone</h2>
  <p>
    When you call trump, you may declare that you'll <strong>go alone</strong>. Your partner sets
    their cards aside and sits out the hand, leaving you to play one-against-two. The reward is large:
    taking all five tricks alone scores <strong>4 points</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Playing the tricks</h2>
  <p>
    The player to the dealer's left leads the first trick. You <strong>must follow the led suit</strong>
    if you can — remembering that the left bower counts as trump, not as its printed suit. If you
    can't follow, you may play any card, including a trump. The highest trump wins the trick; if no
    trump is played, the highest card of the led suit wins. The winner leads the next trick.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring</h2>
  <p>At the end of the hand, score based on the makers' tricks:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Makers take 3 or 4 tricks:</strong> 1 point.</li>
    <li><strong>Makers take all 5 (a "march"):</strong> 2 points.</li>
    <li><strong>Makers go alone and take all 5:</strong> 4 points.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Makers take fewer than 3 (they're "euchred"):</strong> the <em>other</em> team scores
      <strong>2 points</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How you win</h2>
  <p>
    Hands continue, with the deal rotating, until a team reaches <strong>10 points</strong>. Because
    being euchred hands two points to the opponents and a lone march grabs four, momentum can flip in
    a single hand — which is exactly what makes Euchre so quick and tense.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
